WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.944
Rust's ownership model is built around
responsibility.

2
00:00:03.024 --> 00:00:03.890
A value has an owner,

3
00:00:03.970 --> 00:00:07.076
and the owner is responsible for cleanup
when the value goes out of scope.

4
00:00:07.156 --> 00:00:09.295
This is why ownership is not just a syntax
concept.

5
00:00:09.375 --> 00:00:11.921
It is how Rust represents who has
authority over a resource.

6
00:00:12.001 --> 00:00:14.292
When ownership moves, responsibility moves
with it.

7
00:00:14.372 --> 00:00:17.580
A value passed into a function that takes
ownership is no longer owned by the

8
00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:18.017
caller.

9
00:00:18.097 --> 00:00:21.152
A value moved into a struct field is now
part of that struct's lifecycle.

10
00:00:21.232 --> 00:00:24.796
The previous binding is not allowed to
continue behaving as though it still owns the

11
00:00:24.876 --> 00:00:25.182
value.

12
00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:27.910
Borrowing is equally important for
double-free prevention.

13
00:00:27.990 --> 00:00:30.587
A borrowed reference can read or modify a
value temporarily,

14
00:00:30.667 --> 00:00:32.602
but it does not take cleanup
responsibility.

15
00:00:32.682 --> 00:00:36.144
That distinction lets code use values
without creating another owner that might

16
00:00:36.224 --> 00:00:37.905
later try to release the same resource.

17
00:00:37.985 --> 00:00:40.887
Function signatures should make cleanup
responsibility visible.

18
00:00:40.967 --> 00:00:43.004
A function that accepts `T` may take
ownership.

19
00:00:43.084 --> 00:00:45.223
A function that accepts `&T` borrows for
reading.

20
00:00:45.303 --> 00:00:48.358
A function that accepts `&mut T` borrows
for controlled modification.

21
00:00:48.438 --> 00:00:51.748
A function that returns `T` gives
ownership back to the caller or transfers it

22
00:00:51.828 --> 00:00:52.184
onward.

23
00:00:52.264 --> 00:00:54.658
Defensive Rust design avoids ambiguous
cleanup paths.

24
00:00:54.738 --> 00:00:58.098
Do not make callers guess whether a helper
closes a handle, consumes a wrapper,

25
00:00:58.178 --> 00:00:58.993
or only borrows it.

26
00:00:59.073 --> 00:01:00.499
The more sensitive the resource,

27
00:01:00.579 --> 00:01:04.092
the more valuable it is for ownership
transfer to be explicit in the type signature

28
00:01:04.172 --> 00:01:05.700
and documented in the API contract.
